ate onion stat mid-tempo Gondo a TV had  introduced the Corrado as Volkswagens  first real sports car  the new models interior was adapted  partly from the new Passat 3 while much  of the platform technology was taken  from the second-generation VW golf the  modular design ensured high quality  while keeping assembly cost down dick  the ad says the Corrado buy Volkswagens  this is how we imagine a sports car  [Music]  folks Christophe explains the VW added a  few technological highlights into the  Corrado mix hoping to create a marketing  image of the Corrado as a high-tech  vehicle it boasted the most powerful  engine in VW's history with 1.8 liters  for cylinders and a G ladder  supercharger putting out 160 horsepower  and to keep all four wheels on the  ground it was graced with a retractable  rear spoiler like the new Porsche 911 so  the fastest way to draw admiring glances  was to drive up to the village disco in  a Corrado g60  but christoph isn't sure that was the  precise image VW was aiming for the  apostate but even he's a gutsy Jam  listing at $42,500 the VW corrado cost  about as much as a 5 Series Beemer or a  Mercedes e-class out of reach for most  of its young target buyers when  purchased secondhand many Corrado's were  chewed up by tuners and customizers  widened lowered and souped up  joining Kristoff doesn't think the new  Corrado g60 needed any of that a car  reviewer once wrote between 1500 and  6300 revs and moves ahead at near  relentless speed this was mainly because  the G ladder scroll type supercharger  gave the car plenty of pickup at lower  revs bands at leap from 0 to 100 km/h in  8.3 seconds  topping out at 225 incredibly fast for a  front-wheel drive at that time if  Christoph blatantly over does it the car  might understeer a little but he just  has to ease off the gas to get it back  on track in the curves he's having fun  [Music]  once Christophe knows that at the time  VW's marketing department was very proud  of their new rear spoiler concept it  extends automatically when the speed  hits 120 km/h and when you drive down to  the ice-cream parlor and want to flawed  it a little you can put it up manually  but in other respects the Corrado's look  reflects many elements of VW's design  language in the 1990s clear lines smooth  surfaces and rounded corners a little  boring and Christoph's you optically he  definitely wouldn't call it a milestone  the compact VW corrado made up for its  pedestrian design with extra  practicality the big hatchback allowed  easy access to the spacious cargo space  the rear seats accommodated two adult  passengers and the generous standard  equipment package was unmatched at the  time with ABS power steering sports  seats thermal insulated glass and heated  wing mirrors  [Music]  ace does highlight highlight for  Christoph is this red g60 emblem  indicating the legendary G letter  supercharger under the hood it's a  belt-driven compressor that creates  higher gauge pressure even at low revs  and provides a steady increase in thrust  without the infamous turbo lag the major  downside says Christoph is that if you  floor it with the oil temperature to low  or after a do-it-yourself tuning it can  total the G ladder one reviewer wrote at  the time that it's a lot of fun to drive  but takes a toll on your nerves the  turbocharged 1.8 liter four-cylinder  engine puts out a very respectable 160  horsepower in the late 1980s that was  enough to keep up with most Porsches  Christophe tells us the VW was very  proud of its new showcase car but it  didn't achieve the hope for market  success even if the practical 4-cylinder  aspirated engines and somewhat more  impressive vr6 would have satisfied most  demands only about ninety seven thousand  Corrado's were ever built way too few by  VW standards  in the end the Corrado's high-price were  its doom but not before it had set new  benchmarks as a practical high quality  sports car with robust standard  technology that was nearly unparalleled  at the time kristef winds up by saying  Volkswagen might not have earned much  cash off the Corrado but the boost to  the image of a brand seen as boring and  utilitarian was invaluable  the Corrado showed the world that VW  could be more and compact  high-performance vehicles became a  fixture of the manufacturers model range  special features like the G lauder and  the automatic rear spoiler keep the  Corrado on classic car fan radar even  now he says it was definitely a  milestone in automotive history but a  long time would pass before the Corrado  had an official successor 12 years to be  exact and 2008 Volkswagen finally rolled  out another sports coupe a in the  Corrado spirit but they decided to call  it the new Scirocco  and next time on drive it from zero to  100 three point four seconds the Porsche  911 gt3 and a high-end hybrid crossover  SUV the Lexus NX 300h  [Music]  you
 

